<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <!--
    GL-26: local folder feed, no registry, no auth (ARCHITECTURE.md "Packaging: local feed").

    Two sources, one deliberately narrow:
      nuget.org  — everything else, the public internet.
      local-feed — ONLY the GiftList package ids, via packageSourceMapping below, never nuget.org.

    WHY packageSourceMapping IS NOT OPTIONAL — dependency confusion, demonstrated, not assumed.
    `BuildingBlocks` and `Identity.Contracts` are both already taken on nuget.org (1.0.0 and
    1.0.14 respectively, the latter pulling in SolarLab.Common.Contracts transitively). With
    nuget.org as the only configured source, `BuildingBlocks Version="0.1.0"` (a floor, not a
    pin — `[0.1.0]` would be the exact-version form, and this project deliberately does not use
    it) resolves to the stranger's 1.0.0 instead of failing, because NuGet floats forward to the
    nearest version satisfying the range. Listing local-feed as a second, *unmapped* source does
    not fix this: a consumer with both sources enabled can still satisfy an exact version from
    either one, so the impostor package stays reachable. Mapping the GiftList ids to local-feed
    exclusively removes nuget.org from consideration for those ids entirely — NuGet resolves a
    package id from the source(s) tied to the *most specific* matching pattern only, and an exact
    id ("BuildingBlocks") or prefix ("BuildingBlocks.*") always beats the fallback "*".

    WHY THE SAME LITERAL VALUE ("../local-feed") WORKS ON THE HOST AND INSIDE THE .NET SERVICE
    CONTAINERS — verified empirically in both places, not assumed (ARCHITECTURE.md "Making the
    local feed visible to containers" for the mounts this depends on):
      - Host: `dotnet restore` resolves a local source's relative path against *this file's own
        directory* — this repo's root. local-feed already sits one level up as a sibling of every
        service repo, so "../local-feed" is correct with zero developer setup.
      - Container: the repo is bind-mounted at /src (this file's directory at runtime) and the
        feed is bind-mounted at /feed — not at a sibling path — so "../local-feed" resolved from
        /src would naively mean /local-feed, which the compose mount never creates. Each
        service's Dockerfile closes that one-line gap: `RUN ln -s /feed /local-feed`, made
        *outside* /src so the runtime bind mount over /src cannot hide it. The symlink is
        dangling until compose's /feed mount exists at container start, which is fine — a symlink
        resolves when it is read, not when it is created. One nuget.config, unbranched, works in
        both places because the filesystem — not the config — is what's made to differ.

    THE APPROACH THAT LOOKS RIGHT AND ISN'T, TESTED AND REJECTED: do not list both paths as two
    separate sources (one named for the host, one for /feed), both mapped to the same GiftList
    ids, expecting "whichever exists wins". NuGet checks the existence of every LOCAL source that
    packageSourceMapping makes a candidate for the id being restored, and fails the restore with
    NU1301 the moment *any* one of them is missing — even when a sibling source mapped to the
    very same pattern would have resolved it. There is no NuGet-native fallback between local
    folder sources; the symlink above is what ensures only one candidate path ever exists per
    environment, so only one is ever consulted.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" />
    <add key="local-feed" value="../local-feed" />
  </packageSources>
  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="local-feed">
      <package pattern="BuildingBlocks" />
      <package pattern="BuildingBlocks.*" />
      <package pattern="GiftLists.Contracts" />
      <package pattern="Identity.Contracts" />
      <package pattern="Reservations.Contracts" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>
</configuration>
